Format: https://www.debian.org/doc/packaging-manuals/copyright-format/1.0/
Upstream-Name: SAAT
Upstream-Contact: sudo-megas <gonzalorodriguez761@gmail.com>
Source: https://github.com/sudo-megas/SAAT
Comment:
 This package ships a PyInstaller one-folder build under /usr/lib/saat. It
 therefore redistributes not only SAAT itself but the Qt 6 libraries, the
 CPython runtime and a substantial set of third-party C libraries that the
 build bundles, all under /usr/lib/saat/_internal. Every one of them is
 covered below.
 .
 The exact set of bundled shared libraries is decided by PyInstaller from
 what the build machine's wheels link against, so it can shift slightly
 between builds. /usr/share/doc/saat/bundled-libraries.txt is generated
 from the actual contents of the package being built and lists every
 shared object shipped, with its SONAME. packaging/check-licences.sh
 fails the build if that manifest contains a library family this file does
 not name, so the two cannot drift apart.
 .
 Qt is bundled as separate shared libraries and dynamically linked, never
 statically linked, which is what the LGPL-3's terms for combined works
 call for. Replacing the bundled Qt is a matter of substituting the
 libQt6*.so.6 files under /usr/lib/saat/_internal.

